# Check Performance When Relevant

Performance work should be tied to a user requirement or a plausible regression in the changed path.

1. Identify where time, memory, network calls, or database work is spent.
2. Avoid repeated expensive work inside loops or frequently called paths.
3. Measure or profile before introducing a complicated optimization.
4. Compare the same workload before and after a performance change when practical.
5. Preserve readability and correctness; document any important tradeoff.

Do not add caching, concurrency, or a new dependency just because it might be faster. If performance was not relevant to the request or affected path, do not perform unrelated optimization.
